Undifferentiated Soft Tissue Swelling / Localized Edema
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Localized pitting or non-pitting soft tissue swelling | Normal venous duplex ultrasound (no DVT) | Absence of systemic volume overload (e.g., heart failure)

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-042
Reason for Visit: Acute unilateral leg swelling causing severe patient distress, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude deep vein thrombosis or acute cellulitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute, localized limb swelling. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out an evolving deep vein thrombosis or deep space tissue infection, preventing potentially fatal pulmonary embolism or sepsis prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My right leg has blown up like a balloon since this morning, and it feels incredibly tight and heavy when I walk.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with localized swelling in a dependent anatomical area (right lower leg extending to the mid-calf). There is a distinct feeling of tightness and heaviness in the affected limb. The patient describes a gradual onset that has worsened throughout the day while sitting at a desk. There is an absence of focal warmth or intense erythema. The patient explicitly denies severe pain, trauma, or recent long-haul travel.
Functional Status: Fully independent; ambulating with a sensation of heaviness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High somatic anxiety regarding blood clots.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 124/78 mmHg | HR: 80 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient appears anxious but is in no cardiopulmonary distress. A review of recent specialist notes reveals a historical presentation of subjective breathlessness, clear lung auscultation, and normal oxygen saturation (functional dyspnea), which was fully investigated and is completely unrelated to the acute leg swelling.
Physical Exam: Lower extremity examination reveals mild pitting edema upon pressure application extending to the mid-calf on the right, with trace edema on the left. There is a complete lack of significant pain or tenderness on deep palpation of the calf (Homan's sign is negative). There is an absolute absence of intense erythema, and the limb is normal temperature. Regional pulses (dorsalis pedis, posterior tibial) are bounding and capillary refill is brisk. The overlying skin is intact without breakdown, weeping, or distinct demarcated borders.
Environmental Observations: Patient's work-from-home setup involves prolonged sitting without a footrest.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient takes an amlodipine-based antihypertensive (a known cause of dependent edema).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Undifferentiated Soft Tissue Swelling / Localized Edema - Status: Acute functional worsening due to prolonged dependency and potential medication effect.
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) - Ruled out clinically due to the complete lack of deep calf tenderness, absence of prominent superficial collateral veins, and bilateral (though asymmetric) nature of the pitting edema.

Acute Cellulitis - Ruled out definitively due to the complete absence of intense focal heat, lack of spreading erythema, and normal body temperature.

Congestive Heart Failure - Ruled out due to clear lung bases, normal JVP, and lack of significant shortness of breath or orthopnea.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden development of unilateral leg swelling mandated an immediate after-hours home visit to urgently exclude a deep vein thrombosis. Telephone triage appropriately identified the severe risk of an impending pulmonary embolus. A thorough physical and vascular examination confirmed painless, cool, pitting edema without signs of deep venous occlusion or bacterial infection. By identifying the swelling as a benign dependent fluid shift (potentially exacerbated by calcium channel blockers) rather than an acute vascular emergency, the clinician safely bypassed a highly disruptive emergency department admission and secured the patient with immediate mechanical offloading strategies.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated mechanical fluid management protocols. The expected investigation results include a normal venous duplex ultrasound ruling out deep vein thrombosis and normal systemic renal/cardiac markers, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis via outpatient investigation.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manually drain the accumulated fluid, lie completely flat on the floor and rest your heels on the seat of the couch for 20 minutes before bed; physically elevating your legs above your heart utilizes gravity to force the trapped fluid back into your central circulation.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: Advised the purchase of fitted compression stockings from the pharmacy tomorrow.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 7 days for a medication review (amlodipine) and a non-urgent venous Doppler ultrasound.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops sudden, sharp chest pain, unexpected shortness of breath, or if the leg suddenly becomes bright red, hot, and agonizingly painful.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; asymmetric swelling must be monitored closely and formally cleared by ultrasound if it fails to resolve with elevation.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves rapid improvement with strict elevation and movement, though it will reliably recur if prolonged sitting is maintained.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if you begin coughing up blood or feel lightheaded, or if the swelling rapidly spreads up your thigh.

